# Linux & Container Security for CI/CD Runners: Kernel Isolation, cgroups & Rootless Execution

---

## 1. Core Linux Kernel Isolation Boundaries

Unlike Virtual Machines (which isolate hardware via a hypervisor and separate guest kernels), containers share the **host Linux kernel** and rely on layered kernel primitives:

| Boundary Layer | Linux Kernel Mechanism | What It Isolates / Protects |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Visibility & Identity** | **Namespaces** (`pid`, `net`, `mnt`, `uts`, `ipc`, `user`, `cgroup`) | Ensures a build container only sees its own processes (`PID 1`), network interfaces, hostname, and mounted filesystem—hiding host processes and other CI jobs. |
| **Resource Exhaustion** | **Control Groups (`cgroups v2`)** | Caps CPU, RAM, disk I/O, and PIDs so a runaway C++ compiler or fork bomb cannot OOMKill the host or neighbor pods. |
| **Privilege Granularity** | **Linux Capabilities** (`CAP_NET_ADMIN`, `CAP_SYS_ADMIN`, etc.) | Splits monolithic `root` power into ~40 discrete permissions. Containers drop dangerous capabilities by default (blocking `mount`, kernel module loading, and raw packet sniffing). |
| **System Call Surface** | **Seccomp (Secure Computing Mode)** | Filters syscalls made to the shared host kernel (default profiles block ~40+ risky syscalls like `kexec_load`, `reboot`, and `unshare`). |
| **File & Process Access** | **Mandatory Access Control (SELinux / AppArmor)** | Enforces kernel-level policy labels regardless of UID—blocking a compromised container process from reading host files even if it breaks out as `root`. |

