## 3. `cgroup v1` vs. `cgroup v2`

Control Groups (`cgroups`) meter and cap host hardware resources (CPU, memory, block I/O, PIDs) for groups of processes. Modern Linux distributions and Kubernetes releases default to **cgroup v2** and phase out v1.

| Feature | `cgroup v1` (Legacy) | `cgroup v2` (Modern Standard) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Hierarchy Model** | **Multiple separate trees:** Each controller (`cpu`, `memory`, `blkio`) has its own independent directory tree (`/sys/fs/cgroup/memory/...` vs `/sys/fs/cgroup/cpu/...`). | **Single unified hierarchy:** Every process belongs to one cgroup tree under `/sys/fs/cgroup/`, with controllers enabled per subtree (`cgroup.subtree_control`). |
| **Rootless Container Support** | **Unsafe / broken:** Delegating cgroup writes to non-root users in v1 risked privilege escalation, forcing rootless containers to skip resource limits. | **First-class & safe:** Officially supports delegating cgroup subtrees to unprivileged users via `systemd` so rootless builds still enforce RAM/CPU caps. |
| **I/O & Page Cache Accounting** | Disconnected—buffered disk writes (`writeback`) were not properly billed to the cgroup's memory limit, causing noisy-neighbor host OOMs. | Unified memory + I/O accounting accurately bills page cache and disk writeback to the container that triggered them. |
| **Contention Metrics (PSI)** | None—only showed raw CPU/RAM usage, not whether tasks were stalled waiting for resources. | **Pressure Stall Information (PSI):** Exposes `cpu.pressure`, `memory.pressure`, and `io.pressure` (supported natively in Kubelet) to detect resource thrashing before OOMKills hit. |
| **Process Termination** | Racy—runtimes had to loop over PIDs to kill a container, risking fork-bomb escapes. | Atomic `cgroup.kill` file—writing `1` kills every process in the container simultaneously in kernel space. |

